# iOS Liquid Glass

For native app UI, follow Apple Human Interface Guidelines and use SwiftUI system
navigation, tab, sheet, and modal patterns. Load `ios-design-system` before
designing or implementing an iOS screen.

- Use `NavigationStack`, system `.sheet`, and a bottom `TabView`; do not imitate
  Android Material, Bootstrap, or admin-dashboard conventions.
- Use SF Symbols and semantic system colors. Support both light and dark modes. In reference-locked recreation, use SF Symbols only when they visibly match the source; otherwise use a matching vector asset.
- Reserve translucent Liquid Glass for chrome such as navigation, toolbars, tab
  bars, floating actions, and sheets. Keep recipe content surfaces solid.
- Keep separators low contrast, respect a 44pt minimum touch target, and use
  natural system-style transitions and springs.
- Product controls must define default, pointer hover where applicable, pressed,
  disabled, loading, and error or success feedback where relevant. In a
  reference-locked reproduction, draw only source-visible or explicitly required
  states and document all other states as unknown.
- After UI work, verify layout alignment, spacing, text hierarchy, icon
  placement, page transitions, and theme consistency against the chosen Apple,
  Figma, or Mobbin reference. When supplied sources are authoritative, they
  override these references and must pass the native-viewport fidelity protocol.
